 "notes": "53 rows, numbered 115 to 167 in an unbroken run, with no rows that weren't plainly stations. Every row has one number, one type (L.H. or L.V.) and one name. The list gives no light characters, heights or other columns.\n\nHow I recorded things:\n- Fragments: each is the printed line with its leading indent and trailing spaces removed. The spaces inside a line (the padding that lines up the names column) are kept exactly. Two rows run onto a second line: 121 Selker (lines 3366-3367) and 126 Skerries Rock (lines 3375-3376). For each of these, the two lines are given as separate fragments, and the keepers are joined into one entry.\n- Names keep the comma or full stop printed after them (\"Langness,\", \"Point of Ayre.\"), the same way your example \"Farn, Inner,\" does. Strip that last character if you want bare names.\n- Keepers keep \"Mr\", \"Mr.\" or \"Messrs\" as printed, and a group under \"Messrs\" stays as one entry. I dropped only the full stop that ends each row.\n- Coast is the group heading without its full stop (\"Isle of Man\", \"N. W. England\", \"North Wales\", \"South Wales\", \"Bristol Channel\", \"South-Western England\"). Place is null everywhere, because no county or locality is printed beside any name. \"Isle of Man\" is only a heading.\n- The \"No. on Map.\" header sits above the number column.\n\nMarks:\n- 39 rows carry *, but the lead-in says returns came from 34. Starred: 117-123, 125-129, 131-142, 144, 146-148, 152-155, 157, 160-164, 166.\n- 130 St Tudwal's has keepers (Messrs Davies and Parsons) but no *. It is also the only named row with a full stop after the name instead of a comma.\n- 144 Usk has * but no keeper named.\n- These 13 have neither a mark nor names: 115, 116, 124, 143, 145, 149, 150, 151, 156, 158, 159, 165, 167.\n- The paragraph after the list (line 3428 onward) thanks some of these observers for sending wings. Its names agree with the list, e.g. \"Rees and Smith (Skerries)\", \"Nicholas (Nash)\".\n\nSpellings copied as printed that a reader may want to check:\n- \"Air.\" (124) is presumably Point of Air/Ayr, Flintshire.\n- \"Chickens Rock\" has no apostrophe.\n- \"Caldy\" is presumably Caldey.\n- \"Nash (E. ),\" has an odd space before the bracket. It probably means Nash East.\n- \"Flatholm\" is printed as one word.\n- \"Sevenstones\" is printed as one word.\n- \"Mr. W. R. Burgess\" (128) is the only \"Mr\" printed with a full stop.\n- \"Henry Clavell\" (122) has no Mr.\n- \"Messrs Havells and Wyness\" (139 Scarweather): \"Havells\" may be a misprint for Howells. Howells appears at the next-door Helwick (138).\n- \"Mr Arthur Ebgar\" (161) may be a misprint for Edgar.\n\nGroupings: Portland Bill, Shambles, Anvil Point, Hurst Castle and The Needles (163-167) are on the Dorset and Hampshire coast. The print still puts them under \"South-Western England\".",
